+# Contributing
+
+Thanks for contributing to `awesome-best-ai-tools-for-small-businesses-2026`.
+
+## Submission Requirements
+
+- The project **must** have a public `github.com` repository URL.
+- The project **must not** be SaaS-only.
+- The project should be relevant to small-business use cases such as support, workflow automation, document search, internal copilots, website chat, or operations.
+- Include the repository license as shown on GitHub.
+- Include the primary language and approximate star count from GitHub.
+- Include a recent activity signal such as the latest commit date or GitHub "updated" date.
+
+## Preferred Entry Format
+
+```md
+#### [Tool Name](https://github.com/owner/repo)
+> **Description:** Short summary based on the GitHub README, including what it does, core features, and why it fits small businesses.
+
+- **GitHub:** [github.com/owner/repo](https://github.com/owner/repo)
+- **Stars:** 1234 ⭐
+- **Language:** Python
+- **License:** MIT
+- **Last Commit:** 2026-01-01
+- **Category:** tag1, tag2
+- **Best for:** specific use case
+```
+
+## Quality Bar
+
+- Prefer established, maintained repositories over abandoned experiments.
+- Be honest about tradeoffs, custom licenses, and category gaps.
+- Do not submit proprietary hosted products unless the entry is specifically for the open source repository.
+- Do not remove existing entries without a clear reason and source-backed justification.
+
+## Pull Requests
+
+- Keep PRs focused.
+- Explain what you added, removed, or corrected.
+- If you update metadata, note the date you verified it.
+
+## Issues
+
+Open an issue if:
+
+- a repository is no longer public
+- the license changed
+- the project became inactive
+- a better category structure is needed
