+## TL;DR
+- **Best workflow builders:** [Dify](https://github.com/langgenius/dify), [Langflow](https://github.com/langflow-ai/langflow), and [Flowise](https://github.com/FlowiseAI/Flowise) are strong starting points for internal copilots, support bots, and automations.
+- **Best self-hosted chat workspaces:** [Open WebUI](https://github.com/open-webui/open-webui), [AnythingLLM](https://github.com/Mintplex-Labs/anything-llm), and [LibreChat](https://github.com/danny-avila/LibreChat) are the most practical options for private team use.
+- **Best knowledge and RAG stacks:** [RAGFlow](https://github.com/infiniflow/ragflow), [DocsGPT](https://github.com/arc53/DocsGPT), and [Haystack](https://github.com/deepset-ai/haystack) are strong fits for document-heavy businesses.
+- **Best support-focused tools:** [Chatwoot](https://github.com/chatwoot/chatwoot), [Rasa](https://github.com/RasaHQ/rasa), and [Botpress](https://github.com/botpress/botpress) cover helpdesk, conversational AI, and GPT-powered assistant use cases.
+- **Best automation agents:** [CrewAI](https://github.com/crewAIInc/crewAI), [Bytebot](https://github.com/bytebot-ai/bytebot), and [Agent Zero](https://github.com/agent0ai/agent-zero) help teams automate repetitive research and desktop-heavy workflows.
+
+## Why This List
+For many teams, the best AI tools for small businesses are not the loudest SaaS products but the open source building blocks they can inspect, adapt, and run on their own infrastructure. This list filters for public GitHub repositories with real community traction, clear business use cases, and enough activity to be relevant in 2026.
+
+All entries were checked against GitHub repository data. When a project uses a custom or mixed license, that is called out directly instead of being softened or hidden.
